2005-09-24\\n---\\nLate-night television in the United States is the block of television programming intended for broadcast after 11:00 p.m. and usually through 2:00 a.m. Eastern and Pacific Time (ET/PT), but which informally can include programs aired as late as the designated overnight graveyard slot.\\n\\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s7313\",\"d2\",379,757,\"By definition, late night programming begins on the Big Three television networks (ABC, NBC and CBS) at or shortly before 11:35 p.m. ET/PT, after the conclusion of local late-evening newscasts on their owned-and-operated and affiliated stations; late night programming on other broadcast networks, including Fox and PBS, and cable television channels start at 11:00 p.m. ET/PT. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s7314\",\"d2\",757,1038,\"Some streaming services (such as Netflix, Hulu and Amazon Prime Video) have ventured into the late-night talk format at various times in recent years, though these programs are in-format-only, given that episodes are often released before the start of the designated time period.\\n\\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s7315\",\"d2\",1038,1417,\"The following is a list of programs that are currently airing or have previously aired during the late night daypart on American television networks and streaming services.\\n\\nCurrent\\n\\n**Table 1**\\n\\n| Network | Program title | Format | Duration | Days | Time (ET) | Currenthost(s)/anchor(s) | Debut | Length of run |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| ABC | Jimmy Kimmel Live! \",\"document\",[]],[\"s7316\",\"d2\",1417,1736,\"| Talk show | 60 minutes | Monday–Friday | | Jimmy Kimmel | January 26, 2003 | |\\n| ABC | Nightline | Newsmagazine | 30 minutes | Monday–Friday | | Byron Pitts,Juju Chang | March 24, 1980 | |\\n| ABC | World News Now | Overnight newscast | 90 minutes | Monday–Friday | | Andrew Dymburt,Rhiannon Ally | January 6, 1992 | |\\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s7317\",\"d2\",1736,2124,\"| CBS | The Late Show with Stephen Colbert | Talk show | 60 